<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baal Hammon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ba'al Hammon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Hammon was the chief god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Punic world, worshipped with the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a>. His sanctuaries, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tophet">tophets</a>, contain urns of burnt infants and animals whose interpretation as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a> is still debated. After the Roman conquest his cult continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as that of Saturn, the leading god of the African countryside until the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baal-hammon.jpg" alt="Baal Hammon" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_statue_of_Baal-Hammon_on_a_throne_AvL.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
